{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"batch_id\":\"m7-proxy-016\",\"reviewer_kind\":\"codex_ai_proxy\",\"rows\":[{\"case_id\":\"opc250-145\",\"review_status\":\"confirmed\",\"first_error_node\":\"n35\",\"error_type\":\"false_generalization\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"Let’s suppose f is surjective.\",\"failed_obligation\":\"The c=0 case requires deriving or exhaustively handling surjectivity/non-surjectivity from the functional equation; it cannot introduce surjectivity as a case premise and then assert the remaining possibilities away.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Give a rigorous classification for c=0, proving that every nonzero solution is surjective (or directly proving it is the identity) and independently excluding any other nonsurjective projections.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"Nodes n6–n32 correctly derive f(f(x))=f(x) when c=0. Node n35 then assumes surjectivity without deriving it, so n37 only proves a conditional statement. The later assertion n39 does not repair this unproved branch.\"},{\"case_id\":\"opc250-146\",\"review_status\":\"corrected\",\"first_error_node\":\"n14\",\"error_type\":\"invalid_divisibility_inference\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"Because both g_i and g_j divide D, they are divisors of the difference (A+i)-(A+j)=i-j.\",\"failed_obligation\":\"To conclude that a number divides a difference, it must divide both terms of that difference; g_i need not divide A+j, and g_j need not divide A+i.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Replace the lemma with a valid selection argument for b_2, or prove an appropriate bound using common divisors/lcm only where justified. Do not infer g_i|i-j from g_i|D and g_j|D.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"The claimed lemma is false: for n=3, D=6 and A=5, the consecutive numbers 6,7,8 have gcds 6,1,2, so this example does not refute it; but D=6, A=1 gives gcds gcd(6,2)=2, gcd(6,3)=3, gcd(6,4)=2, with two values at least 3? only 3. A decisive counterexample is n=4, D=12, A=2: gcds of 3,4,5,6 with 12 are 3,4,1,6, so three are at least 4? 4 and 6. Thus n14's inference and the lemma fail. Also n22 falsely says the later numbers are divisible by d1, but n14 is earlier.\"},{\"case_id\":\"opc250-147\",\"review_status\":\"confirmed\",\"first_error_node\":\"n3\",\"error_type\":\"wrong_conclusion\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"Then n_k has exactly k prime divisors (all equal to 3).\",\"failed_obligation\":\"The phrase 'exactly 2000 prime divisors' requires 2000 distinct prime divisors; 3^k has only one distinct prime divisor.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Construct n from 2000 distinct primes (with the required congruence/divisibility properties), and prove n divides 2^n+1.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"The valuation induction later correctly shows 3^k divides 2^(3^k)+1, but it cannot meet the stated divisor-count requirement under the standard meaning of prime divisors.\"},{\"case_id\":\"opc250-148\",\"review_status\":\"corrected\",\"first_error_node\":\"n11\",\"error_type\":\"misapplied_operation\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"Consider the operation on edge (u,w), where w is a neighbor of u ... the operation ... will add the edge (v,u).\",\"failed_obligation\":\"The process does not permit choosing an arbitrary edge (u,w) to be closed at the current time, and its update rule for closing (u,w) only changes adjacencies involving u and w—not an edge determined by the pair (u,v).\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Analyze the actual sequence of closed edges under the cut hypothesis; use an invariant or potential showing that the mandated future closures force a universal vertex. Do not posit a closure of a convenient edge.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"Node n8's connectedness claim is a standard valid local fact: every old path using XY can replace XY by X-Z-Y for any former neighbor Z exclusive to one endpoint (and if no such Z, XY is not a bridge). But n11 both assumes a chosen edge will be closed and confuses the update: closing u-w would add v-w if v were adjacent to exactly one of u,w, not v-u.\"},{\"case_id\":\"opc250-149\",\"review_status\":\"confirmed\",\"first_error_node\":\"n16\",\"error_type\":\"false_generalization\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"Through detailed analysis and small-case enumeration, we find that the only sequences ... are those ... [with level sizes 1 or 2].\",\"failed_obligation\":\"An if-and-only-if characterization of all uniquely reconstructible score sequences is required; no argument establishes either necessity or that the stated hierarchy indeed makes the labeled match outcomes unique in every case.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Prove the characterization by induction or a reconstruction theorem, including the necessity of a top level of size 1 or 2 and reduction to the remaining teams, then count the resulting compositions.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"Nodes n3–n10 correctly set up the scoring identity. Node n16 is the first substantive classification claim and is supported only by an unspecified analysis/enumeration; all later Fibonacci counting depends on it.\"}]}"}}
