{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\Wong2\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw 'C:\\\\Users\\\\Wong2\\\\.codex\\\\skills\\\\algebra-obligation-checker\\\\SKILL.md'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\Wong2\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -Raw 'C:\\\\Users\\\\Wong2\\\\.codex\\\\skills\\\\algebra-obligation-checker\\\\SKILL.md'\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: algebra-obligation-checker\ndescription: Dependency-guided obligation checking for natural-language algebra proofs. Use when an AI coding agent needs to build a proof dependency graph, retrieve theorem-bank rules, distinguish repairable gaps from invalid reasoning, identify the first problematic node, and propose a minimal repair.\n---\n\n# Algebra Obligation Checker\n\n## Overview\n\nUse this skill to audit algebra proofs with a dependency graph and local proof obligations. The goal is not to fully formalize the proof, but to produce a structured trace that separates proof gaps from genuine invalid reasoning.\n\nThis skill follows the portable Agent Skills directory layout. It can be used\nby Codex, Claude Code, Gemini CLI, or another agent that can load `SKILL.md`.\nIgnore `agents/openai.yaml` on clients that do not use OpenAI UI metadata. Read\n`references/compatibility.md` when installing or invoking the skill on a new\nCLI or operating system.\n\nWhen deterministic checking leaves a possible proof gap, read and apply\n`references/gap_completion_standard.md`. Gap classification is delegated to\nthe AI completion stage under that standard; sentence splitting alone is not\nevidence of a mathematical gap.\n\nFor calculation nodes, read and apply\n`references/calculation_adjudication_standard.md`. The checker owns and\npropagates the active axiom context; the calculation model proposes atomic\nsteps and may use only axioms exposed by that context.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the proof instance as theorem, assumptions, and ordered proof steps.\n2. If the problem contains at least two explicit sequential subquestion labels,\n   split it and check the subquestions in order. Do not split unlabeled adjacent\n   questions. Add accepted nodes from each earlier subquestion to a temporary\n   theorem bank for later subquestions.\n3. If the proof is given as a single text block, use `split_proof_into_nodes()` to\n   decompose it into individual node-level claims (see Node Splitting Rules below).\n4. Convert each proof step into one graph node.\n5. Before checking any node, give the theorem, assumptions, and complete\n   ordered node list to one Graph Builder pass. Require exactly one result per\n   node containing its direct earlier dependencies and a self-contained claim.\n   Validate the complete DAG as specified in `references/data_contract.md`.\n6. For each node, create the local obligation from theorem assumptions and\n   only its validated direct dependencies:\n\n```text\nGiven assumptions and earlier accepted claims, prove the current claim.\n```\n\n7. Retrieve relevant rules from the theorem bank.\n8. Classify the node with the fixed status labels in `references/data_contract.md`.\n9. Record gap, invalid, and undetermined locations separately.\n10. Run the Iterative Repair Procedure (see below) to generate repairs for all\n    problematic nodes in order, inserting bridge steps for gaps and identifying\n    irreparable errors.\n\n## Iterative Repair Procedure\n\nThe repair procedure scans proof nodes sequentially and attempts to fix each\nproblematic node. It maintains an ordered list of accepted claims (initial\ntheorem assumptions plus all nodes processed so far, including inserted bridge\nsteps) and produces a repaired proof with minimal inserted steps.\n\n### Step 1: Classify the node error\n\nRead the node's `status` from the diagnosis output:\n\n- `missing_bridge_lemma` �� the node is correct but omitted a standard argument.\n- `missing_assumption`, `theorem_misuse`, `algebraic_invalidity`, `false_theorem` �� the node contains a non-gap error.\n\n### Step 2A: Repair a gap (missing_bridge_lemma)\n\nIf the node is a `missing_bridge_lemma`:\n\n1. Identify the node's direct dependency claims from `depends_on`. These are\n   the conditions already established before this node.\n2. Identify the node's `self_contained_claim` as the target conclusion.\n3. Construct a short step-by-step derivation that starts from the dependency\n   claims and ends at the target conclusion. Each step must be a single\n   mathematical inference with an explicit justification (rule, definition,\n   earlier claim, or assumption). The derivation should read like a natural\n   self-contained solution to the subproblem:\n\n   ```text\n   Dependencies (conditions) |- Target conclusion\n   ```\n\n4. Insert the generated derivation steps **between** the dependency nodes and\n   the current node in the proof sequence. These inserted steps become part of\n   the accepted claims for subsequent node checking.\n5. Update `first_gap_step` if this was the first unresolved gap.\n6. **Continue** to the next node (go to Step 1) �� the repair is local and\n   does not affect the validity of later nodes.\n\n> Example: If node 3 claims `x = y` and depends on node 1 (`a != 0`) and\n> node 2 (`a*x = a*y`), the repair generates:\n> ```\n> Inserted step 3a: Since a != 0, a has a multiplicative inverse a^{-1}.\n> Inserted step 3b: Multiply both sides of a*x = a*y by a^{-1}:\n>                   a^{-1}*(a*x) = a^{-1}*(a*y).\n> Inserted step 3c: By associativity, (a^{-1}*a)*x = (a^{-1}*a)*y,\n>                   i.e., 1*x = 1*y, so x = y.\n> ```\n> Node 3 then becomes `closed` with these inserted steps as its justification.\n\n### Step 2B: Handle a non-gap error\n\nIf the node has a non-gap error (`missing_assumption`, `theorem_misuse`,\n`algebraic_invalidity`, or `false_theorem`):\n\n1. Determine whether the node's `self_contained_claim` is derivable from the\n   **original problem conditions** (the theorem statement + the theorem\n   assumptions), ignoring any missing local premises. Use your mathematical\n   judgment: is the claim mathematically true under the problem's global\n   hypotheses?\n\n2. **If the claim is NOT derivable** from the problem conditions:\n   - Stop the repair loop immediately.\n   - Report this node as **irreparable**.\n   - Set `first_invalid_step` to this node's index.\n   - Emit a clear diagnosis explaining why the claim cannot be salvaged under\n     the given conditions. Do not proceed to later nodes.\n\n3. **If the claim IS derivable** from the problem conditions:\n   - Generate a step-by-step derivation from the problem conditions to the\n     target conclusion. This derivation should:\n     - Use the original theorem assumptions as the starting point.\n     - Refer to earlier accepted proof nodes wherever they are helpful\n       (to avoid redundant reasoning).\n     - Fill in any missing premises or rule applications that the original\n       proof step omitted.\n     - Each step must be a single inference with explicit justification.\n   - Insert the derivation steps before the current node, replacing the\n     original faulty step.\n   - The node becomes `closed` after repair.\n   - **Continue** to the next node (go to Step 1).\n\n### Step 3: Continue or terminate\n\n- If the node was repaired (Step 2A or Step 2B case 3), move to the next node\n  and return to Step 1.\n- If an irreparable error was found (Step 2B case 2), terminate the repair\n  loop and return the partial repair result with the error location.\n- If all nodes are processed, return the fully repaired proof with all\n  inserted bridge steps in their correct positions.\n\n### Repair invariants\n\n1. Inserted bridge steps must be minimal: do not add steps that are already\n   explicit in the original proof or that are logically redundant.\n2. The repaired proof order must be consistent: inserted steps go immediately\n   before the node they repair, after that node's declared dependencies.\n3. A repaired gap does not change the original node's status in the\n   `proof_graph` �� create a separate `inserted_steps` list in the output to\n   record the new steps. The output format is:\n\n```text\n{\n  \"repaired_proof\": [\n    {\"type\": \"original\", \"node_id\": 1, \"claim\": \"...\"},\n    {\"type\": \"original\", \"node_id\": 2, \"claim\": \"...\"},\n    {\"type\": \"inserted\", \"repairs_node\": 3, \"step\": \"a\", \"claim\": \"...\"},\n    {\"type\": \"inserted\", \"repairs_node\": 3, \"step\": \"b\", \"claim\": \"...\"},\n    {\"type\": \"original\", \"node_id\": 3, \"claim\": \"...\"},\n    ...\n  ],\n  \"repair_outcome\": \"fully_repaired\" | \"partial_with_error\",\n  \"first_irreparable_error\": null | {\"node_id\": 3, \"reason\": \"...\"}\n}\n```\n\n4. For non-gap errors repaired from problem conditions, the derivation should\n   still use intermediate results from earlier accepted proof nodes wherever\n   applicable, to keep the repair minimal and connected to the existing proof\n   structure.\n\n11. If the helper emits unresolved adjudications, complete them as the active\n    host agent, rerun the helper with those responses, and use only the\n    validated resumed result.\n\n## Explicit Subquestions\n\nSplit only when the problem has at least two sequential line-leading labels,\nsuch as `(1)/(2)`, `(a)/(b)`, `1./2.`, or `Part 1/Part 2`. Two consecutive\nquestions without labels remain one proof instance. Inputs may provide\n`problem_text` plus `proof_text`, or an `explicit_subquestions` array. See\n`references/data_contract.md` for the structured form.\n\nAfter checking a subquestion, convert its `closed`, `valid_with_gap`, and\n`missing_bridge_lemma` nodes into temporary `DerivedRule` entries. Add those\nentries only to the working theorem bank for later subquestions of the same\nparent problem. Never write them into the persistent theorem-bank file.\n\n## Node Splitting Rules\n\nWhen the input proof is a continuous text (not pre-split into steps), use the\n`split_proof_into_nodes()` function to decompose it. The function applies the\nfollowing rules in order:\n\n### Base Rule\nSplit the text at sentence boundaries: periods `.`, exclamations `!`, question\nmarks `?`, semicolons `;`, and newlines. Each resulting segment is a candidate\nnode.\n\n### Cause & Effect Rule (English_Logical_Connectors.pdf �� Category 1)\nIf a Cause & Effect word appears **mid-sentence** (not at the very start of a\nsegment), split the segment into two nodes **before** that word.\n\nCause & Effect words include:\n`therefore`, `thus`, `consequently`, `hence`, `accordingly`, `as a result`,\n`as a consequence`, `because`, `since`, `so`, `thereby`, `due to`, `owing to`,\n`lead to`, `result in`, `give rise to`, `stem from`, `arise from`\n\n### Condition Rule (English_Logical_Connectors.pdf �� Category 7)\nIf a segment starts with a Condition word, that word begins a new node.\nContinue merging subsequent segments into this condition node until a\nconjunction (a linking word from any logical connector category) is\nencountered. Split a new node **before** that conjunction.\n\nCondition words include:\n`if`, `unless`, `provided that`, `providing that`, `as long as`, `so long as`,\n`on condition that`, `in case`, `in the event that`, `suppose that`,\n`supposing that`, `otherwise`, `or else`, `whether`\n\nConjunctions that terminate a condition node include all Cause & Effect words\nplus: `but`, `however`, `nevertheless`, `nonetheless`, `whereas`, `while`,\n`although`, `though`, `even though`, `yet`, `still`, `moreover`, `furthermore`,\n`in addition`, `additionally`, `besides`, `also`, `then`, `finally`, `first`,\n`second`, `third`, `next`, `lastly`, `in conclusion`, `in summary`,\n`for example`, `in particular`, `in fact`, `actually`, `indeed`,\n`in other words`, etc.\n\n### Comma Rule (English_Logical_Connectors.pdf �� all categories)\nScan each segment left-to-right. For each comma NOT inside parentheses (math\nnotation), check whether the text between the last sentence boundary (or\nsegment start) and this comma contains a logical connector word. If yes,\nsplit at this comma: the text from the previous boundary through the comma\nbecomes its own node.\n\nWill NOT split at:\n- Commas inside parentheses or brackets (protects math notation like\n  `d(en, x)` or `(a, b)`)\n- Commas in number lists (protects `i = 1, 2, ..., N`)\n\n### Subject-Predicate Merge Rule\nAfter all splitting phases, validate every node for basic subject-predicate\nstructure. Nodes that are fragments (e.g., bare conjunctions like \"then\",\nprepositional phrases like \"for each n in N,\") get merged with an adjacent\nnode to form a complete statement:\n\n- Node starts with punctuation (`,`, `;`, `:`, `.`, `!`, `?`) �� merge with\n  **next** node\n- Node ends with punctuation but has no subject/predicate �� merge with\n  **previous** node (or with **next** if it's the first node)\n- No punctuation at either end �� merge with **next** node\n\nThe merge concatenates the two nodes with a space, removing the separator\nbetween them.\n\n### Period Split Rule\nAfter the merge phase, re-scan every node for period-separated sentence\nboundaries (`. ` or `.\\n`). Try to split at each period, but ONLY if both\nresulting pieces would have subject + predicate (or math-operator-equivalent)\nstructure. If either piece is a fragment, do NOT split.\n\nThis re-introduces sentence-level granularity that was lost during merge,\nwhile preventing orphaned fragments like `\"If a = 0,\"` from becoming\nstandalone nodes.\n\n> Example: `\"Hence E_degree cap E^c_bar = emptyset. Assume for contradiction that p notin E_degree cup E^c_bar.\"`\n> �� The right piece `\"Assume...\"` has SP (has math relation `in`). Split:\n>   `[\"Hence E_degree cap E^c_bar = emptyset.\", \"Assume for contradiction that p notin E_degree cup E^c_bar.\"]`\n>\n> Example: `\"Therefore x_n = a^n x_0 + b(1-a^n)/(1-a). If a = 0,\"`\n> �� The right piece `\"If a = 0,\"` is a conditional fragment �� NO split.\n\n## Node Classification Rules\n\nAfter splitting, classify each node into exactly one of the following five\ncategories:\n\n### 1. Definition (����)\n\nA node that **defines a new mathematical term or symbol** for the first time.\nThis is limited to cases where a node explicitly introduces and defines a new\nconcept, notation, or function.\n\n**Keywords / patterns**:\n- `\"X is the set of ... such that ...\"`�����弯��/���\n- `\"Define X to be ...\"`\n- `\"Let A := ...\"`��������ѧ���ţ�\n- `\"X is called ... if ...\"`\n- `\"We say that ...\"`\n- `\"Let ... denote ...\"`������Ǻţ�\n- `\"Let f(x) = ...\"`�����庯����\n\n> ? `\"E_degree is the set of points of E such that a neighborhood of this point is a subset of E.\"` �� **definition**������\"�ڲ�\"�����ѧ���\n>\n> ? `\"Let A := lim sup a_n and B := lim sup b_n.\"` �� **definition**��������� A, B��\n>\n> ? `\"Let f(x) = sqrt(2 + sqrt x).\"` �� **definition**�����帨������ f��\n>\n> ? `\"there is a sequence {xi} in E' that converges to x\"` �� **NOT definition**��������Ӧ�ü��޵㶨�壬���Ƕ����¸��\n>\n> ? `\"Let d be the metric on E.\"` �� **introduction**��������Ŀ������\n\n### 2. Introduction (����/����)\n\nA node that **restates information that is already known** �� either from the\nproblem statement, from existing theorems, or from previously proven steps in\nthe proof. Introduction does not add new mathematical reasoning, it only\nbrings existing information into the current context.\n\n**Includes the following**:\n\n#### a) ������Ŀ���� (Restating problem conditions)\nRestating the hypotheses or givens of the theorem being proved.\n\n**Keywords / patterns**:\n- `\"Let d be the metric on E\"`��������Ŀ˵��\"E is a metric space\"��\n- `\"Let {G_alpha} be an open cover of K.\"`��������Ŀ�еĿ����ǣ�\n- �ڷ����и������������\n\n> Example: `\"Let d be the metric on E.\"` �� **introduction**\n>\n> Example: `\"Let {G_alpha} be an open cover of K.\"` �� **introduction**\n\n#### b) ���ö��� (Citing theorems)\nReferencing an existing theorem, lemma, or known mathematical fact.\n\n**Keywords / patterns**:\n- `\"We recall from the monotone convergence property that ...\"`\n- `\"According to Lemma 2.1, ...\"`\n- `\"By the triangular inequality, ...\"`\n- `\"We have proven previously that ...\"`\n- `\"since ...\"`���� since �������**������ʵ**���Ǽ���������ʱ����������֪���塢��������֤���ۣ�\n\n> Example: `\"We recall from the monotone convergence property that a monotonic bounded sequence is convergent.\"` �� **introduction**\n>\n> Example: `\"We have proven previously that the limit points of E' are limit points of E.\"` �� **introduction**\n\n#### c) ������֤���� (Referencing proven results)\nReferring back to a conclusion that was already established earlier in the\nsame proof.\n\n**Keywords / patterns**:\n- `\"Since ... is arbitrarily chosen / taken,\"`��������������\n- `\"Since {G_n} is taken arbitrarily,\"`\n- `\"Since the sum on the right is not infty + infty, A and B must have the same sign.\"`��������֪��ʵ������\n\n> Example: `\"Since l is arbitrarily taken,\"` �� **introduction**\n>\n> Example: `\"Since f is arbitrarily chosen,\"` �� **introduction**\n\n#### d) �������/�Ǻ� (Introducing notation)\nIntroducing temporary notation or variables for the purpose of the proof.\n\n**Keywords / patterns**:\n- `\"Use l to denote the limit of {ln}.\"`\n- `\"Take e in E_degree.\"`\n- `\"For each epsilon > 0, ...\"`\n- `\"Then (i)\"`, `\"Then (ii)\"`��������Ŀ��\n- `\"For example if E = {1/n},\"`, `\"A counterexample is s_n = (-1)^n.\"`\n\n> Example: `\"Use l to denote the limit of {ln}.\"` �� **introduction**\n>\n> Example: `\"Take e in E_degree.\"` �� **introduction**\n\n#### e) ���������� (Reductive declarations) �� NOT claim\n\nThese patterns are **introduction** because they restate the proof strategy\nrather than assert a substantive claim:\n\n- `\"It suffices to show that ...\"`\n- `\"It is enough to prove that ...\"`\n- `\"It remains to show that ...\"`\n- `\"We need only prove that ...\"`\n- `\"The problem reduces to proving that ...\"`\n\n> Example: `\"It suffices to show E_degree sqcup E^c_bar = X, where X is the universal set.\"` �� **introduction**\n>\n> Example: `\"Hence it suffices to show that {s_i} is bounded and monotonic.\"` �� **introduction**\n\n### 3. Assumption (����)\n\nA node that states a hypothesis, condition, or premise �� either the theorem's\ngiven assumptions or a case-distinction assumption introduced mid-proof.\n\n**Keywords / patterns**:\n- `\"If ...\"`, `\"If ... then ...\"`������\"then\"���ֱ�����һ�ڵ�ʱ��\n- `\"Assume ...\"`, `\"Assume for contradiction that ...\"`����֤����\n- `\"Suppose ...\"`\n- `\"Case N: ...\"`����������ۣ�\n- `\"Otherwise ...\"`�����������\n\n> Example: `\"If x is a limit point of E',\"` �� **assumption**\n>\n> Example: `\"Assume for contradiction that p in E_degree cap E^c_bar.\"` �� **assumption**\n>\n> Example: `\"Case 1: A and B are both not infinity.\"` �� **assumption**\n\n### 4. Claim (����)\n\nA node that declares a statement which requires subsequent reasoning to be\nestablished. The claim is the **goal** of the upcoming sub-proof, not a fact\nthat follows directly from the preceding step.\n\nOnly the following three subtypes of���� are classified as **claim**:\n\n#### a) ֱ��ʽ���� (Direct Declaration)\nPattern: subject + ���涯�� + that�Ӿ�\n\n| Pattern | Examples |\n|---------|----------|\n| **We show that** + ���� | *\"We show that Q^k is a countable dense subset.\"* |\n| **We prove that** + ���� | *\"We prove that the set is closed.\"* |\n| **We claim that** + ���� | *\"We claim that this sequence converges.\"* |\n| **We assert that** + ���� | |\n| **We maintain that** + ���� | |\n| **We will show that** + ���� | |\n| **We shall prove that** + ���� | |\n| **We now prove that** + ���� | |\n| **We first establish that** + ���� | |\n| **We have proven that** + ���� | ������\"��֤\"��Ϊ�˺���ʹ�ö���ֱ�ӿ��Ƴ��� |\n\n���Σ�we֮���subject����\n- `\"For boundedness: We show by induction that s_n < 2.\"` �� **claim**\n- `\"For monotonicity: We show monotonicity via induction.\"` �� **claim**\n\n#### b) Ŀ������ (Goal Declaration)\nPattern: Ŀ���Զ��� + ���涯�� + that�Ӿ�\n\n| Pattern | Examples |\n|---------|----------|\n| **Our goal is to show that** + ���� | |\n| **We aim to prove that** + ���� | |\n| **Our aim is to establish that** + ���� | |\n| **What we need to prove is that** + ���� | |\n| **The claim to be proved is that** + ���� | |\n| **We wish to prove that** + ���� | |\n\n#### c) �ڶ�ʽ���� (Sectional Declaration)\nPattern: �����Զ��� + ���涯��\n\n| Pattern | Examples |\n|---------|----------|\n| **To proceed, we first establish the following:** | |\n| **As a first step, we show that** + ���� | |\n| **We first prove a lemma:** | |\n| **We begin by showing that** + ���� | |\n| **Before proving the main result, we first show that** + ���� | |\n| **The proof should be separated into several cases.** | *\"The proof should be separated into several cases.\"* |\n\n#### ?? Excluded: ���������� (Reductive Declaration) �� NOT claim, classify as **introduction**\n\nThese patterns reduce the original problem to a sub-problem, which is a proof\nstrategy rather than a substantive claim:\n\n| Pattern | Examples |\n|---------|----------|\n| **It suffices to show that** + ���� | *\"It suffices to show that {s_i} is bounded and monotonic.\"* |\n| **It is enough to prove that** + ���� | |\n| **It remains to show that** + ���� | |\n| **We need only prove that** + ���� | |\n| **To prove X, it suffices to prove that** + ���� | |\n| **The problem reduces to proving that** + ���� | |\n\n> ? **claim** examples:\n> - `\"We show that Q^k is a countable dense subset.\"`\n> - `\"We then show that {I_u} is an at-most countable set of disjoint open intervals whose union is U.\"`\n> - `\"For boundedness: We show by induction that s_n < 2.\"`\n> - `\"The proof should be separated into several cases.\"`\n>\n> ? **NOT claim** (these are **introduction**):\n> - `\"It suffices to show E_degree sqcup E^c_bar = X, where X is the universal set.\"`\n> - `\"Hence it suffices to show that {s_i} is bounded and monotonic.\"`\n\n### 5. Calculation Step (���㲽��)\n\nA node that performs **multi-step algebraic manipulation, inequality\nderivation, limit evaluation, or symbolic transformation** �� moving from one\nexpression to the next through a reasoning chain.\n\n**Not a calculation**: A single inequality or equation statement without a\nderivation chain (e.g. `\"a < b\"`, `\"x = 0\"` alone), as well as single\nconstructive or existence assertions (e.g. `\"We select ...\"`,\n`\"We let N such that ...\"`, `\"We can take ...\"`). These are conclusions,\nnot calculation steps.\n\n**Keywords / patterns**���������Ƶ�������:\n- ����������: `\"... = ... = ...\"`, `\"... is equivalent to ...\"`\n- ����ʽ�Ƶ���: `\"... le ... + ... < ...\"`�������ǲ���ʽ��չ����\n- ����/չ��: `\"By recursion, ... = ...\"`, `\"From X, we have Y = ...\"`\n- Ӧ�ö������Ƶ�: `\"Then, according to Lemma 2.1, we have ...\"`\n- ȡ����: `\"Take n to +infty to obtain ...\"`\n- ���ɲ��Ƶ�: `\"If s_n < 2, due to monotonicity of f, we have s_{n+1} = f(s_n) < ...\"`\n\n**Distinction from Claim**: A calculation step is self-contained �� the\ntransformation follows from known rules/algebra and does not set up a\nmulti-step sub-proof.\n\n> ? `\"Hence d(en, x) le d(en, yn) + d(yn, x) < 2^{-n} for each n in N,\"` �� **calculation step**�����ǲ���ʽ�Ƶ�����\n>\n> ? `\"Taking r = r0 in (i) would lead to a contradiction with (ii).\"` �� **calculation step**���Ƶ�ì�ܣ�\n>\n> ? `\"This is equivalent to |a| le |a - b| + |b| (1) and |b| le |b - a| + |a| (2).\"` �� **calculation step**���ȼ۱����Ƶ���\n>\n> ? `\"B(p, r) cap E^c = emptyset\"`����һ����ʽ/��ʽ���� **���� calculation**��ͨ����Ϊ conclusion\n\n### 6. Conclusion (����)\n\nA node that draws a deductive conclusion from the preceding steps. The\nconclusion follows logically from what has already been established.\n\n**Keywords / patterns**:\n- `\"Hence ...\"`, `\"Therefore ...\"`, `\"Thus ...\"`\n- `\"Consequently ...\"`, `\"As a result, ...\"`\n- `\"We deduce that ...\"`, `\"This implies that ...\"`\n- `\"So ...\"`����������ʱ��\n- ì�ܽ���: `\"... contradiction.\"`\n- `\"which contradicts ...\"`\n- ���յĴ𰸳���: `\"No.\"`, `\"Yes.\"`������Ϊ�ش�ʱ��\n\n#### Catch-all rule\n\n**If a node does not fit any of the above categories (definition, introduction,\nassumption, claim, calculation step), classify it as conclusion.**\n\nThis is the default / fallback category. A node falls here when it:\n- States a result that follows from the previous reasoning but does not match\n  the specific keywords of the���� patterns\n- Is a standalone declarative statement that cannot be assigned to any other\n  category\n- Simply reports an answer or a fact that is presented as the output of the\n  proof step\n\n> Example: `\"Therefore any limit point of E' is an element of E',\"` �� **conclusion**\n>\n> Example: `\"Hence E_degree cup E^c_bar = X.\"` �� **conclusion**\n>\n> Example: `\"K is compact.\"` �� **conclusion**\n>\n> Example: `\"No.\"` �� **conclusion**���ش����⣬�Ҳ����������κ����\n\n### Classification Priority\n\nWhen a node could match multiple categories, use this hierarchy:\n\n1. **definition** �� if directly defining a mathematical term\n2. **claim** �� if it matches any of the three���� patterns (ֱ��ʽ/Ŀ��ʽ/�ڶ�ʽ)\n3. **assumption** �� if it is a conditional/hypothetical premise\n4. **introduction** �� if it restates known information (problem conditions, theorems, past results, notation, proof strategy)\n5. **calculation step** �� if it performs algebraic or logical manipulation\n6. **conclusion** �� **default / catch-all** for anything that doesn't fit above\n\nThis priority ensures that:\n- ���� patterns take precedence over their content keywords (e.g., \"We show that K is compact\" is a **claim**, not a **conclusion**)\n- Nodes that don't match any specific pattern automatically fall to **conclusion**\n\n### CLI Usage\nLocate this skill directory through the active client's skill registry. Invoke\nthe bundled Python script without assuming a repository-relative skill path.\nUse `--raw-proof` to pass raw proof text directly:\n\n```text\npython <skill-dir>/scripts/check_obligations.py --input <input.jsonl> --theorem-bank <rules.jsonl> --output-dir <output-dir> --raw-proof \"Since a is nonzero, a has an inverse. Therefore x = y.\"\n```\n\n## Data Contract\n\nRead `references/data_contract.md` before changing schemas, prompts, or checker scripts. The canonical result schema is:\n\n```text\nschemas/algebra_obligation_result.schema.json\n```\n\n## Script\n\nRun the portable first pass from any operating system with Python 3.9 or newer:\n\n```text\npython <skill-dir>/scripts/check_obligations.py --input <input.jsonl> --theorem-bank <rules.jsonl> --output-dir <output-dir> --emit-adjudication-template <pending.json>\n```\n\nIf `<pending.json>` contains entries, fill each `response` directly as the\nactive host agent. The helper emits only the earliest unresolved node per proof\nin each round so that later contexts are not stale. A proof-level `graph`\nentry is emitted first and must be completed from the entire supplied node\nlist; its `node_id` is `0`. Use\n`references/gap_completion_standard.md` for `proof` entries and\n`references/calculation_adjudication_standard.md` for `calculation` entries.\nResume with:\n\n```text\npython <skill-dir>/scripts/check_obligations.py --input <input.jsonl> --theorem-bank <rules.jsonl> --output-dir <output-dir> --adjudications <round-1.json> --adjudications <round-2.json>\n```\n\nDo not stop after emitting pending obligations. Do not invoke a separate model\nAPI merely to replace the active host agent. Missing or invalid responses must\nremain `undetermined`. Repeat emit, adjudicate, and resume until no pending\nentry remains or a round makes no progress. Pass every completed prior-round\nfile when resuming so earlier validated context is preserved.\n\nThe portable path uses only the Python standard library. Paths are handled\nwith `pathlib`, so `